<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Animated figure: assets/sphere-patch-manim.html (three.js, in the     -->
<!-- manim style of tetrahedron-manim.html), right after fig-sphere-patch. -->
<!-- Its SKELETAL switch is on here: the normal is shown only as the unit  -->
<!-- normal n-hat, and neither r_phi x r_theta nor the area of the patch   -->
<!-- is named, since both are blanks in the text.                         -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Asymptote (Oct 2026): the plate in a flow, the sphere patch, the      -->
<!-- cylinder with its two normals and the paraboloid cap and disk         -->
<!-- (fig-flux-plate, fig-sphere-patch, fig-cylinder-normals,              -->
<!-- fig-paraboloid-flux) are drawn in Asymptote, as in the completed      -->
<!-- notes: to scale and rotatable on the web page. The unit normal of the -->
<!-- sphere patch is magenta and n2 of the cylinder orange (no green). The -->
<!-- TikZ originals are kept in retired-figures.txt.                       -->
<section xml:id="section-surface-integrals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Surface Integrals: Flux Through a Surface</title>

<introduction>
<p>
How much fluid crosses a surface per unit time? For a flat surface in a
constant velocity field the answer is a single dot product; for a curved
surface in a varying field it is a <term>surface integral</term>. In
this section we build the surface integral in three steps: we learn how
to parametrize a surface, how to use the parametrization to find a
normal vector, and how to combine the two to compute the flux of a
vector field.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-flux-flat">
<title>Flux Through a Flat Surface</title>

<p>
Suppose water flows with constant velocity <m>\mathbf{v}_0</m> through
a flat surface <m>S</m> of area <m>A</m> that is perpendicular to the
flow. How much water passes through <m>S</m> in one second, and in what
units? Now tilt the surface, so that the flow meets it at an angle
<m>\theta</m> away from the normal direction, as in
<xref ref="fig-flux-plate"/>: only the component of the velocity
perpendicular to the surface pushes water through it. Write the flow
rate, and then rewrite it as a dot product of <m>\mathbf{v}_0</m> with
a normal vector <m>\mathbf{n} = \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,A</m> whose length is
the area of <m>S</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the flow rate through the plate.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-flux-plate">
<caption>
A tilted plate <m>S</m> of area <m>A</m> in a uniform flow
<m>\mathbf{v}_0</m>. Only the component of <m>\mathbf{v}_0</m> along
the normal <m>\mathbf{n}</m> carries fluid through the plate.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-flux-plate" width="70%">
<shortdescription>
A tilted plate in a uniform flow, with its normal vector and the angle
between the normal and the velocity marked.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A blue parallelogram-shaped plate <m>S</m> stands tilted in a uniform
flow of parallel gray velocity arrows <m>\mathbf{v}_0</m> pointing up
and to the right. A magenta arrow <m>\mathbf{n}</m>, normal to the
plate, makes an angle <m>\theta</m> with the velocity arrows.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// A tilted plate S of area A in a uniform flow v0, with its normal n and
// the angle theta between n and v0.
import graph3;

size(11.5cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={115}{18}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.8619, 0.4019, 0.309));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

pen oiblue    = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oimagenta = rgb(204/255, 0/255, 119/255);

// uniform flow: parallel gray arrows in the direction (0, 1, 0.35)
pen flowpen = gray(0.65) + linewidth(0.9pt);
for (real xx : new real[] {-0.9, 0.1, 1.1}) {
  for (real zz : new real[] {-1.4, 0, 1.4}) {
    draw((xx, -2.2, zz)--(xx, -0.9, zz + 0.45), flowpen, Arrow3(size=6));
    draw((xx, 0.6, zz + 0.55)--(xx, 1.9, zz + 1.0), flowpen, Arrow3(size=6));
  }
}

// the plate: a parallelogram whose normal lies in the y-z plane
path3 plate = (-1.1, -0.7, 0.6)--(1.1, -0.7, 0.6)--(1.1, 0.7, -0.6)--(-1.1, 0.7, -0.6)--cycle;
draw(surface(plate), surfacepen=material(0.45*oiblue + 0.55*white + opacity(0.75),
                                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue,
                                         specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(plate, oiblue + linewidth(1.2pt));
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit((0.8619, 0.4019, 0.309));
label("$S$", (-0.7, -0.35, 0.35) + toviewer, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));

// the normal n and the velocity v0 at the center of the plate
triple n = (0, 0.85, 0.99), v = (0, 1.3, 0.455);
draw(O--n, oimagenta + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=9));
label("$\mathbf{n} = \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,A$", n, 1.2N, oimagenta + fontsize(15pt));
draw(O--v, gray(0.15) + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=9));
label("$\mathbf{v}_0$", v, E, fontsize(15pt));

// the angle theta between them
draw(arc(O, 0.56*unit(n), 0.56*unit(v)), gray(0.35) + linewidth(0.8pt));
label("$\theta$", 0.78*unit(unit(n) + unit(v)), fontsize(14pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Our goal is to generalize the above scenario in two respects: (I) we
generalize the surface <m>S</m> (not just a plane), and (II) we
generalize the vector field (not just a constant). To achieve this
goal, we will need to learn: (I) how to parametrize a surface
<m>S</m>; (II) how to use the parametrization of <m>S</m> to find the
normal vector <m>\mathbf{n}</m>; and (III) how to use (I) and (II) to
compute the flux of a vector field. Watch
<xref ref="fig-flux-plate-video"/> for the flat-plate picture.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-flux-plate-video">
<caption>
Flow through a flat plate: perpendicular flow, and then the plate
tilted by an angle <m>\theta</m>. Use it to check the flow rate you
wrote down.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-flux-plate"
       source="videos/flux_flat_plate_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-parametric-surfaces">
<title>Parametric Surfaces</title>

<p>
Recall how we parametrize curves in the <m>xy</m>-plane: the parabola
<m>y = x^2</m> is traced by
<m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle t, t^2 \rangle</m> for <m>t \in \R</m>;
only one parameter is needed. A surface is two-dimensional, so its
parametrization needs <em>two</em> parameters.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-parametric-surfaces">
<title>Parametrizing Surfaces</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find parametric representations for each of the following surfaces.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Parametrizing Surfaces.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-parametric-surfaces-video">
<caption>
Parametrizing the paraboloid <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>, the sphere of
radius <m>2</m>, and the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>: two parameters
sweep out coordinate grids on each surface.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-parametric-surfaces"
       source="videos/parametric_surfaces_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-surface-normal">
<title>The Normal Vector to a Surface</title>

<p>
Consider the unit sphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m> with its spherical
parametrization
</p>

<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-sphere-param" number="yes">
\mathbf{r}(\theta,\phi) = \underline{\hspace{3.2cm}}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Our goal is to find the normal <m>\mathbf{n}</m> to the sphere at every
point on its surface. Holding one parameter fixed and varying the
other produces two tangent vectors,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf{r}_\phi \amp = \frac{\partial \mathbf{r}}{\partial \phi}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \lim_{\Delta\phi \to 0}
\frac{\mathbf{r}(\theta, \phi + \Delta\phi)
- \mathbf{r}(\theta,\phi)}{\Delta\phi},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\mathbf{r}_\theta \amp = \frac{\partial \mathbf{r}}{\partial \theta}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \lim_{\Delta\theta \to 0}
\frac{\mathbf{r}(\theta + \Delta\theta, \phi)
- \mathbf{r}(\theta,\phi)}{\Delta\theta},
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
so that the two edges of a small coordinate patch on the sphere are
approximately <m>\mathbf{r}_\phi\, \Delta\phi</m> and
<m>\mathbf{r}_\theta\, \Delta\theta</m>, as in
<xref ref="fig-sphere-patch"/>. Use them to approximate a normal vector
to the patch and the area of the patch:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf{n} \amp \approx \underline{\hspace{3.2cm}},
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-normal-patch" number="yes">
\Delta\sigma \amp \approx \underline{\hspace{3.2cm}}.
</mrow>
</md>

<figure xml:id="fig-sphere-patch">
<caption>
A small coordinate patch on the unit sphere. The tangent vectors
<m>\mathbf{r}_\theta</m> and <m>\mathbf{r}_\phi</m> span the patch,
and <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> is the unit normal.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-sphere-patch" width="80%">
<shortdescription>
A wireframe piece of a sphere with two tangent vectors and the normal
vector drawn at a point.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A wireframe octant of the unit sphere. At a point
<m>\mathbf{r}(\theta,\phi)</m> on the surface, a blue tangent
vector <m>\mathbf{r}_\theta</m> points east along a parallel, a red
tangent vector <m>\mathbf{r}_\phi</m> points south along a
meridian, a small shaded patch <m>\Delta\sigma</m> lies between them,
and a magenta arrow <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> points radially outward,
perpendicular to both.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// A small coordinate patch on the unit sphere, with the tangent vectors
// r_theta and r_phi and the outward unit normal n-hat at r(theta, phi).
import graph3;

size(12cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={115}{18}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.8619, 0.4019, 0.309));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oimagenta    = rgb(204/255, 0/255, 119/255);

// a point of the sphere from (theta, phi), theta from the x-axis and
// phi from the z-axis
triple sph(real t, real p) { return (sin(p)*cos(t), sin(p)*sin(t), cos(p)); }

// the octant of the unit sphere, lightly shaded, with its wireframe
triple octpt(pair q) { return sph(q.y, q.x); }    // param: (phi, theta)
draw(surface(octpt, (0, 0), (pi/2, pi/2), 6, 6, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(gray(0.85) + opacity(0.2), emissivepen=gray(0.3),
                         specularpen=gray(0.05)));
pen wire = gray(0.65) + linewidth(0.4pt);
for (int k = 1; k <= 8; ++k) {
  real p = radians(10k);
  draw(arc((0, 0, cos(p)), (sin(p), 0, cos(p)), (0, sin(p), cos(p))), wire);
}
for (int k = 0; k <= 6; ++k) {
  real t = radians(15k);
  draw(arc(O, Z, (cos(t), sin(t), 0)), wire);
}

// the patch theta in [35, 55] deg, phi in [45, 65] deg
// drawn a hair outside the sphere so that it does not flicker against it
triple patchpt(pair q) { return 1.012*sph(q.y, q.x); }  // param: (phi, theta)
draw(surface(patchpt, (radians(45), radians(35)), (radians(65), radians(55)), 2, 2,
             Spline),
     surfacepen=material(gray(0.45) + opacity(0.6), emissivepen=gray(0.2),
                         specularpen=gray(0.05)));

// the base point r(theta, phi) at theta = 35 deg, phi = 45 deg
real t0 = radians(35), p0 = radians(45);
triple r = sph(t0, p0);
triple rt = sin(p0)*(-sin(t0), cos(t0), 0);            // r_theta
triple rp = (cos(p0)*cos(t0), cos(p0)*sin(t0), -sin(p0));  // r_phi
draw(O--r, gray(0.2) + linewidth(1.1pt), Arrow3(size=7));
dot(r, linewidth(4pt));
draw(r--r + 0.45*rt, oiblue + linewidth(1.5pt), Arrow3(size=8));
draw(r--r + 0.45*rp, oivermillion + linewidth(1.5pt), Arrow3(size=8));
draw(r--1.55*r, oimagenta + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=9));

// labels
label("$\mathbf{r}(\theta,\phi)$", 0.45*r, 2W, fontsize(14pt));
label("$\mathbf{r}_\theta$", r + 0.45*rt, E, oiblue + fontsize(16pt));
label("$\mathbf{r}_\phi$", r + 0.45*rp, SE, oivermillion + fontsize(16pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", 1.55*r, NE, oimagenta + fontsize(16pt));
label("$\Delta\sigma$", 1.12*sph(radians(45), radians(55)), fontsize(14pt));

// axes
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), O--1.5X, axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), O--1.5Y, axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), O--1.35Z, axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
dot((0, 0, 1.6), invisible);
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-sphere-patch-manim"/> is the same patch, animated: move
the point around the sphere, shrink the patch and follow it in, and
watch it flatten onto the parallelogram spanned by its two edges.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-sphere-patch-manim">
<caption>
The coordinate patch of <xref ref="fig-sphere-patch"/>, animated. The
point <m>\mathbf{r}(\theta,\phi)</m> sits on its parallel (blue), along
which <m>\theta</m> varies with velocity <m>\mathbf{r}_\theta</m>, and
on its meridian (red), along which <m>\phi</m> varies with velocity
<m>\mathbf{r}_\phi</m>. The yellow patch is cut out by steps
<m>\Delta\theta</m> and <m>\Delta\phi</m>; the violet parallelogram,
spanned by its edges <m>\mathbf{r}_\phi\,\Delta\phi</m> and
<m>\mathbf{r}_\theta\,\Delta\theta</m>, approximates it, and the green
arrow is the unit
normal <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-sphere-patch-manim"
             iframe="sphere-patch-manim.html"
             width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
The <m>\theta</m> and <m>\phi</m> sliders move the point around the
sphere and down from the north pole, and the patch slider changes
<m>\Delta\theta</m> and <m>\Delta\phi</m> together.
<q>Parallelogram</q> switches the parallelogram spanned by the two edges
off and on,
<q>Normal</q> shows the unit normal <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, and <q>Zoom to patch</q> follows the patch in so
that it fills the view as it shrinks: make it small and the curved patch
and the flat parallelogram become one. <q>Play</q> runs a guided tour,
dragging the figure turns it, <q>Spin</q> turns it on its own, and
<q>Full screen</q> fills the screen with it.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
Carry out this computation for the sphere: find
<m>\mathbf{r}_\phi</m>, <m>\mathbf{r}_\theta</m> and their cross
product, then the unit normal, and say in words which way it points.
Compare with <xref ref="fig-surface-normal-video"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,13.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the normal to the unit sphere.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-sphere-normal">
<title>The Normal to the Unit Sphere</title>
<p>
For the parametrization <xref ref="eq-sphere-param"/> of the unit
sphere,
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \mathbf{r}_\phi \times \mathbf{r}_\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \underline{\hspace{3.8cm}}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-sphere-cross" number="yes">
\amp = \underline{\hspace{3.8cm}},
</mrow>
</md>
and the unit normal is
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-sphere-unit-normal" number="yes">
\amp \hat{\mathbf{n}}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \underline{\hspace{3.8cm}}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \underline{\hspace{3.8cm}}.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<figure xml:id="fig-surface-normal-video">
<caption>
The tangent vectors <m>\mathbf{r}_\theta</m> and
<m>\mathbf{r}_\phi</m> span a small patch of the sphere; their cross
product is normal to the surface and its length measures the patch
area.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-surface-normal"
       source="videos/surface_normal_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-oriented-surfaces">
<title>Oriented Surfaces</title>

<p>
For a <term>closed surface</term>, that is, a surface that is the
boundary of a solid region <m>E</m>, the convention is that the
<term>positive orientation</term> is the one for which the normal
vectors point <em>outward</em> from <m>E</m>, and inward-pointing
normals give the negative orientation.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-cylinder-orientation">
<title>Two Orientations of a Cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>. Describe its two possible
orientations.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,4.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for describing the two orientations of the cylinder and how they are related.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylinder-normals">
<caption>
The cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> with its two possible orientations,
<m>\mathbf{n}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{n}_2</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-cylinder-normals" width="55%">
<shortdescription>
A cylinder around the vertical axis with an outward normal arrow and
an inward normal arrow at the same point.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent cylinder of radius one around the <m>z</m>-axis. At a
point on its curved surface, a magenta arrow <m>\mathbf{n}_1</m>
points horizontally away from the axis, and an orange arrow
<m>\mathbf{n}_2</m> points from the same point horizontally toward
the axis.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The cylinder x^2 + y^2 = 1 with its two orientations at one point:
// n1 away from the z-axis and n2 = -n1 toward it.
import graph3;

size(9.5cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={115}{18}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.8619, 0.4019, 0.309));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

pen oiblue    = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oiorange  = rgb(230/255, 159/255, 0/255);
pen oimagenta = rgb(204/255, 0/255, 119/255);

// the cylinder, z from -1.2 to 1.4 (param: angle, z; normal points out)
triple wallpt(pair q) { return (cos(q.x), sin(q.x), q.y); }
draw(surface(wallpt, (0, -1.2), (2pi, 1.4), 16, 1, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.4*oiblue + 0.6*white + opacity(0.2),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(circle((0, 0, 1.4), 1), 0.6*oiblue + 0.4*white + linewidth(0.8pt));
draw(circle((0, 0, -1.2), 1), 0.6*oiblue + 0.4*white + linewidth(0.8pt));

// axes
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-1.6, 0, 0)--(1.6, 0, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -2.6, 0)--(0, 2.6, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, -1.5)--(0, 0, 1.9), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));

// the two normals at the point (0, 1, 0.2)
triple P = (0, 1, 0.2);
dot(P, linewidth(4pt));
draw(P--(0, 2.0, 0.2), oimagenta + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=9));
label("$\mathbf{n}_1$", (0, 1.85, 0.32), N, oimagenta + fontsize(16pt));
draw(P--(0, 0.15, 0.2), oiorange + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=9));
label("$\mathbf{n}_2$", (0, 0.42, 0.34), N, 0.75*oiorange + fontsize(16pt));

// the equation, to the left of the cylinder on the page
label("$x^2+y^2=1$", 1.05*(0.4226, -0.9063, 0) + (0, 0, 0.9), W, oiblue + fontsize(16pt));
dot((0, 0, 2.15), invisible);
dot(2.4*(0.4226, -0.9063, 0) + (0, 0, 0.9), invisible);  // room for the equation
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-surface-integral-def">
<title>The Surface Integral of a Vector Field</title>

<p>
The following definition should be compared to the definition of the
line integral of a vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> along a curve
<m>C</m>, i.e. <m>\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{T}\, ds</m>, where
<m>\mathbf{T}</m> was defined as the unit tangent vector to the curve
<m>C</m>. For a surface, the unit tangent is replaced by the unit
normal.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-surface-integral">
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is a continuous vector field defined on an
oriented surface <m>S</m> with unit normal vector
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, then the <term>surface integral of
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> over <m>S</m></term> is
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-flux-def" number="yes">
\amp \quad = \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
This integral is also called the <term>flux</term> of
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> across <m>S</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<remark xml:id="remark-flux-param">
<p>
If the surface <m>S</m> is parametrized with respect to <m>u</m> and
<m>v</m>, we can write the unit normal vector as
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \dfrac{\mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_v}
{\lVert \mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_v \rVert}</m>, which can be
combined with the fact that
<m>d\sigma = \lVert \mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_v \rVert\,
du\, dv</m> (from <xref ref="eq-normal-patch"/>) to write the surface
integral as
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-flux-param" number="yes">
\amp = \underline{\hspace{4cm}},
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
where <m>R</m> is the region of the parameters in the
<m>uv</m>-plane. The normalizing length cancels against the area
factor, which is what makes this formula so computable.
</p>
</remark>

<p>
For instance, if the vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is the velocity
field of a fluid, then the surface integral of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> over
the surface <m>S</m> is the net rate at which fluid is crossing
<m>S</m> in the chosen positive direction. As another example, if the
vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> represents the electric field due to a
charged particle such as an electron, then the surface integral of
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> over the surface <m>S</m> corresponds to the flux of
the electric field through the surface <m>S</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-sphere-flux">
<title>Flux Across the Unit Sphere</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the flux of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} +
x\,\mathbf{k}</m> across the unit sphere
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,12.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Flux Across the Unit Sphere.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-sphere-flux-video">
<caption>
The field <m>\mathbf{F} = z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} +
x\,\mathbf{k}</m> crossing the unit sphere. Use it to check the flux
you computed.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-sphere-flux"
       source="videos/sphere_flux_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-paraboloid-flux">
<title>Flux Across a Closed Surface</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate the surface integral
<m>\displaystyle \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
d\sigma</m>, where
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} +
z\,\mathbf{k}</m> and the surface <m>S</m> is the boundary of the
solid region <m>E</m> enclosed by the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and the plane <m>z = 0</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,17);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,16.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the example Flux Across a Closed Surface.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-paraboloid-flux-video">
<caption>
The closed surface made of the paraboloid cap <m>S_1</m> and the disk
<m>S_2</m>, with outward normals. Use it to check the flux of
<m>\mathbf{F} = y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
through each piece.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-paraboloid-flux"
       source="videos/paraboloid_flux_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-paraboloid-flux">
<caption>
The boundary <m>S</m> of the solid enclosed by
<m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and <m>z = 0</m>: the cap <m>S_1</m> and the
disk <m>S_2</m>, with outward normals.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-paraboloid-flux" width="75%">
<shortdescription>
A paraboloid dome over a disk, with outward normal arrows on the dome
and downward normal arrows on the disk.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent blue dome, the paraboloid <m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>,
labeled <m>S_1</m>, sits over the unit disk in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
labeled <m>S_2</m>. Magenta arrows on the dome point up and outward,
and magenta arrows below the disk point straight down: together they
give the closed surface its positive, outward orientation.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The boundary S of the solid enclosed by z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 and z = 0:
// the cap S1 with upward-and-outward normals and the disk S2 with
// downward normals.
import graph3;

size(11.5cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={115}{18}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.8619, 0.4019, 0.309));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

pen oiblue    = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oimagenta = rgb(204/255, 0/255, 119/255);

// the cap S1 (param: radius, angle; normal points up and out)
triple cappt(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), 1 - q.x^2); }
draw(surface(cappt, (0, 0), (1, 2pi), 4, 16, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.45*oiblue + 0.55*white + opacity(0.5),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
// the disk S2, lightly shaded
draw(surface(circle(O, 1)), surfacepen=material(gray(0.8) + opacity(0.45),
                                                emissivepen=gray(0.3),
                                                specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(circle(O, 1), oiblue + linewidth(1.2pt));

// axes
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-1.6, 0, 0)--(1.6, 0, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -1.9, 0)--(0, 1.9, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, -0.7)--(0, 0, 1.7), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));

// outward normals on the cap, n = (2x, 2y, 1), and at the apex
pen npen = oimagenta + linewidth(1.3pt);
for (real t : new real[] {20, 110, 200, 290}) {
  real c = Cos(t), s = Sin(t);
  triple p = (0.7c, 0.7s, 0.51);
  draw(p--p + 0.32*(1.4c, 1.4s, 1), npen, Arrow3(size=7));
}
draw((0, 0, 1)--(0, 0, 1.45), npen, Arrow3(size=7));
// downward normals under the disk
for (real t : new real[] {45, 165, 285}) {
  triple p = (0.55*Cos(t), 0.55*Sin(t), 0);
  draw(p--p - (0, 0, 0.5), npen, Arrow3(size=7));
}

// labels, to the left of the cap and of the disk on the page
triple left = (0.4226, -0.9063, 0);
label("$S_1$", 0.85*left + (0, 0, 0.6), W, oiblue + fontsize(17pt));
label("$S_2$", 1.08*left + (0, 0, -0.12), W, fontsize(17pt));
dot((0, 0, 1.95), invisible);
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

